{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 160,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Anydesk for sponsoring PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to quantum physics, the universe should \nhave collapsed on itself in the instant after the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8000,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Big Bang due to all particles being 100 million \nbillion times heavier. But recent observations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the universe existing suggest that this \nmay not have happened. Even more recent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18720,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observations at the Large Hardron Collider \nhave failed to find out why. In particular,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why the Higgs boson is light enough to allow the \nuniverse to exist. This is part of something known  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31080,
    "dDurationMs": 12640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as the hierarchy problem, and some consider it to \nbe the most important unsolved problem in physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43720,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone, quick update before \nwe start! We've observed that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47440,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "YouTube algorithm isn’t promoting our \nepisodes within the community as much,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so we’re conducting an experiment. Over \nthe next few episodes, we’ll test whether  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58440,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increasing likes and comments impacts sharing. If \nyou'd like to be a data point in this experiment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65440,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hit like and/or leave a comment. \nAnd if you’re new and enjoying this,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70600,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subscribe and hit the bell. We’ll analyze the \nresults and report back after a few episodes!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77440,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Standard Model of particle physics is a \npretty humble name for one of the pinnacle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82160,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "achievements of 20th century physics. It takes \nthe mathematical formalism of quantum field theory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87800,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and uses it to describe every known particle \nin the universe. But when first developed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93880,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the standard model had a pretty major issue. \nIn its early form, none of the particles,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99200,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not even the matter particles, had any mass. \nA particle without mass is obliged to travel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at exactly the speed of light all the time \nand never slower. The Standard model predicted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111200,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a universe in which all particles acted like \nradiation, which would imply no atoms or what we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117720,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "think of as matter. Maybe such a universe exists \nout there, but it’s not this one. Apparently."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The particles of matter—the electrons, quarks, \netc. do have mass—so the builders of the standard  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131080,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "model needed a mechanism to give it to them. And \nthe best candidate for the job was a mechanism  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simultaneously developed by several people \nin the early 60s. That mechanism is driven  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142480,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by what we now call the Higgs field, after \none of those several. With that field also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "came the inevitability of a new particle—the \nHiggs boson. Now, if we could just detect this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154520,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle we would verify this mass-granting \nmechanism and wrap up the standard model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160840,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the large hadron collider was built, fired up,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165160,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and in 2012 the Higgs boson obediently \nappeared with the right mass to make sense  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the masses of the other known particles. \nNice work! Standard Model achieved! Of course,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mysteries still remained—dark matter, dark energy, \nthe matter-antimatter asymmetry, neutrino masses,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183440,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how gravity fits into the picture. Quite \na bit actually. But the discovery of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188840,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Higgs really did close out the Standard \nModel as a fully self-consistent theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193760,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There was one particular detail to sort \nout with regards to the Higgs. An i to dot,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so to speak. If the Higgs gives mass to the \nparticles of the standard model, what gives the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Higgs its mass? And more importantly, why does it \nhave the specific mass that it does? It turns out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that, just as we needed the Higgs to explain \nthe masses of the Standard Model particles,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216840,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we needed a whole family of new particles to \nexplain why the Higgs has the mass it does. In  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222000,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particular, to explain why it has a low mass, \nin the range of other standard model particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228760,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the LHC kept powering up, physicists \nexpected these new particles to pop out,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235720,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and there was nothing. It’s now been \nwell over a decade since the discovery  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240880,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Higgs Boson and it’s the only new \nelementary particle the LHC has found."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247600,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This seems like a relatively minor \nproblem—nothing a little more theorizing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252600,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can’t fix. But it’s considered such a serious \nissue by many that it’s been called the crisis  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259840,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in physics. Today I want to convey why \nsome think of this as such a big deal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265560,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It all comes down to one questions: why is the \nmass of the Higgs boson small? In particular,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271920,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why is it so much smaller than the current \nversion of the Standard Model predicts it to be?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277160,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This episode is a follow-on from our two recent \nones on the quantum structure surrounding the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282560,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron and how that defines the electron’s mass. \nIf I stop making sense at any point, maybe go  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289680,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "check those out and come back. Or just absorb \nall the science words and hope for the best."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296840,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s go step by step. First, the origin of mass. \nWe covered how the Higgs field grants mass to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles of matter a long time ago. For now \nit’s enough to know that due to the unusual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308840,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nature of the Higgs field, it exists in a state \nof non-zero energy everywhere in the universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315520,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Particles that interact with this field absorb \nsome of that energy and this becomes their mass.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or at least part of it. Once a particle has mass \nfrom the Higgs field, it will also gain even more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326280,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass from the cloud of quantum fluctuations \nthat surrounds the particle. We covered that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331280,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a lot of detail in the last episode in this \nseries. The mass of the electron comes from both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336840,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its so-called bare mass, as well as the mass of \nthe swarm of matter-antimatter pairs flickering  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342160,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around it that lead to quantum corrections to that \nmass, resulting in what we call the dressed mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350880,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those quantum corrections come from literally \nevery quantum field the particle can interact  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355720,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with, and every energy scale that these \ninteractions can happen at. If we just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keep adding up those quantum corrections \nwith no upper limit on the energy scale,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366880,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we get that the particle has infinite \nmass. Particles don’t have infinite mass,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372200,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so what’s up? So, it’s possible to cancel out \nsome of these quantum corrections because some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "add positive energy and some add negative energy. \nIf the quantum corrections are basically random,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385400,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then the cancellation in general won’t be perfect \nexcept by extreme luck. So imagine that we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391800,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discover a particle that has a tiny mass and we \ncan only explain that tinyness as being due to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397280,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many enormous quantum corrections cancelling out \nalmost perfectly by pure chance. That would seem  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403920,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unnatural. The mass should really be around \nthe scale of the largest corrections. We also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "call such an unnatural cancellation as “finely \ntuned” because it seems like someone went in and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416480,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "twiddled the knobs of these otherwise unrelated \nquantum corrections to make the cancellation work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423160,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we see that a particle’s mass looks \nunnaturally small or finely tuned,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we typically assume that we’ve missed something. \nAnd we’re typically right. There are a few cases  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433200,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where we’ve found some sort of new physics that \nenables cancellation of those quantum corrections.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This new physics typically works above a certain \nenergy scale, suppressing quantum corrections  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "above that scale. For a given particle, its mass \nwill still receive contributions from random  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum corrections up to that scale. In general \nthis means the measured mass of any particle will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456120,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be at around the energy scale where that new \nphysics gets started with its cancellation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462640,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of the electron we figured out \nthat most of the mass cancellation comes from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a particular virtual interaction involving the \nelectron’s antimatter counterpart. This works  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473280,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all the way down to the low measured mass of \nthe electron. See the previous episode for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477800,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the deets. An important point is that this works \nfor the electron because it’s a spin-½ fermion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484000,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which means it has an antimatter counterpart \nof assist in this cancellation. More formally,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the electron has an extra symmetry—chiral \nsymmetry—that want to pull the mass to zero,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496120,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just as quantum fluctuations want to increase it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, new physics that appears at a \nparticular energy can give us a way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505000,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to avoid piling on virtual mass up to infinity. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This phenomenon is seen for other particles too. \nThere are a couple of cases involving mesons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514840,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which are combinations of a quark and \nan anti-quark. For example the Kaon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is a strange quark combined with either \nan up or down quark. The masses of the kaon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sub-types are very close to each other. However, \nwhen the kaon was first discovered we tried to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calculate that mass difference and found that \nit should be much larger than measured—and that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "large predicted mass difference was due to \nlarge quantum quantum corrections. This felt  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540040,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unnatural, and so physicists guessed that there \nmust be some unknown physics at a very particular,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545840,
    "dDurationMs": 8600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "low-ish energy in order to keep the kaon masses \nin check. They figured that the energy to be a few  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554440,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "giga-electron volts. Shortly afterwards the charm \nquark was discovered at 1.5 GeV, and adding the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563400,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charm to the quantum correction calculations gave \nus the correct mass difference for the kaon. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569960,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new physics appeared at right around the necessary \nenergy to protect the kaon from being unnatural."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576880,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Something similar is true of the pion, \nanother meson whose mass difference is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580520,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protected by the rho meson, which has exactly \nthe right energy to keep the pion in check."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585600,
    "dDurationMs": 8120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, great, so if the particle mass is small then \nwe expect to find new physics at an energy scale  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not too much greater than the particle’s mass \nin order to suppress large quantum corrections.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600680,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now back to the Higgs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602560,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Higgs boson is particularly vulnerable \nto having its mass blown up because of its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unusual status as the only spin-0 particle in \nthe Standard Model. It doesn’t have the natural  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protection that the electron and quarks have by \nvirtue of their additional symmetries. The Higgs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is its own antiparticle, so it does have the \nsame self-canceling virtual interactions as the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624600,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron, for example. Based on the standard model \nas we currently know it, there is no mechanism to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631320,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protect its low mass at any known energy. Now, \nwe know that the standard model for sure fails  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639800,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at a certain very high energy—that’s the energy \nof the Planck scale—10^17 times more energetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the Higgs boson. This is also the scale at \nwhich we think gravity and spacetime themselves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "become quantum. We don’t know what happens \nabove this scale, but at the very least we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expect fluctuations so energetic that they curve \nthe fabric of spacetime. For sure there could be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664400,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mechanisms above that scale to prevent the \nHiggs mass from becoming literally infinite,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669760,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but before we get to those we expect there to \nbe fluctuations that should drive the Higgs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674840,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass close to the Planck scale. So maybe these \nextreme quantum corrections get eliminated by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681760,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chance cancellations. But the chance to randomly \ntune that cancelation to one part in 100 million  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690040,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billion is low, to say the least. It would be \nthe biggest fine-tuning problem in physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697760,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because of that, physicists expect that other \nnon-random mechanisms are at work at lower  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702800,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy scales to do the necessary cancellations. \nIn the 1970’s, physicists came up with an elegant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system to do just that called supersymmetry, \nor SUSY. SUSY postulates that every particle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both the fermions that constitute matter \nand the boson that transmit forces,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720200,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has a supersymmetric counterpart. For every \nfermion there’s a supersymmetric boson,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725360,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and for every boson a counterpart fermion. If \nthese SUSY particles have the right energies,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730440,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they cleanly cancel out the diverging \nquantum corrections of the Higgs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735680,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And to do the job they’re needed for, the \nlightest of these SUSY particles would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740040,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have had masses not too much larger \nthan the Higgs itself. In which case,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744800,
    "dDurationMs": 9560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the LHC should have found them by now. Which it \ndidn’t. So, what went wrong and what does it mean?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754360,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up, let me say that supersymmetry might \nstill be right. The LHC has probed up to a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particular energy range, and that includes \nthe range where the most parsimonious version  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of SUSY was expected to reveal its first \nparticles. But there could still be SUSY  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771520,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles at higher energies, and those \ncould still serve to protect the Higgs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775280,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass. The problem is that even if LHC finds \nits first SUSY particle in the next year,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781960,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its mass will be too high to completely \navoid some fine-tuning of the Higgs mass.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786880,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This has led to a lot of effort to come up \nwith new theories that explain the low mass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790800,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Higgs. I can’t go into them all now, so \nI’ll just mention a couple of prominent ones."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798160,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are various models that describe the \nHiggs as a composite particle arising from a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803160,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slightly more complicated quantum field than the \nusual Higgs field, but which does the same job  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808360,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of granting mass. This field is fermionic, \nwhich means its excitations have spin 1/2,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814960,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it behaves somewhat like the strong force \nin the way its particles bind via confinement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820600,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in pairs to give the spin-zero composite Higgs. \nBut the mass of this particle is \"dynamical\",  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828840,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaning it arises from the interaction, not as a \nfundamental property of the individual particles.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And those individual particles have their own \nmass protected in the same way as other fermions.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means this composite Higgs is safe from being \nblown up in the same way as an elementary Higgs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846120,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be. This family of theories are called \n\"technicolor\" as a play on the color charge of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852760,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the strong force, although this would really be \na different force and field altogether. The idea  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858400,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a composite Higgs isn't new, and it predates \nthe so-called crisis in physics. In fact, many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864680,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technicolor models were ruled out by the discovery \nof the Higgs, and more have been ruled out since.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870320,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, there are still versions that could \nwork and perhaps solve the hierarchy problem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876920,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe the most controversial solution is that \nit’s genuinely crazily unlikely to have such a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885080,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "small Higgs mass. Maybe those random cancellations \nreally did happen to line up just by chance. Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892680,
    "dDurationMs": 7960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if this were the only universe then indeed this \nwould be a stroke of good fortune. But what if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are many universes, each with slightly \ndifferent physics and so that the Higgs mass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "takes on different values across the multiverse \n? Most of these would have a Higgs particle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without a ginormous mass—these would instantly \nrecollapse. Only a few would last any time at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918480,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there’d be fewer still with a Higgs mass \nand other standard model parameters that were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924440,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amenable to the formation of stars, planets \nand life. And if that’s the case, well which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931240,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe do you think we’d find ourselves in ? \nOne that can produce us, probably. This is the so "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938960,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called the anthropic principle, and it’s a \nperfectly reasonable argument. It’s also strongly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946040,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disliked by many physicists because it means that \nthe whole idea of naturalness sort of dies as a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tool for advancing physics. We’ve talked about the \nanthropic principle before, and will again—it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956480,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a deep subject and often mischaracterized. And \nthat’s why I won’t say any more about it now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962760,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Higgs hierarchy problem is actually just one \nfacet of a more global hierarchy problem - related  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to other extreme differences in the scales of \nnature. The difference between the Higgs mass and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973200,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Planck scale, but also the difference between \nthe strength of gravity and the strength the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977800,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum forces, and the weakness of dark energy \ncompared to the theoretical prediction from,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "again, the expected large quantum fluctuations \napproaching the Planck sale. In upcoming videos  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we'll talk more about the hierarchy problem, and \nwhy it's considered such a deep mystery, and why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it may point the way to deeper physics. In the \nmeantime let's just be grateful for the lightness  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999040,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Higgs boson, which, apparently, did not \nthwart the big bang and recollapse spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005520,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Anydesk for supporting PBS. \nScientists often find themselves in some of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009880,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most remote corners of the world. And whether \nyou’re in a remote location or just working from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014560,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "home, the need for reliable, secure communication \nand data transfer remains important. With Anydesk,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021320,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "file transfer functionality allows for \nfast, secure, and direct file transfers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1026080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bypassing the cloud entirely. With coverage all \nover the world Anydesk remains fast and reliable,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with over 99% uptime. And when you’re \nworking on that groundbreaking research,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036480,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you don’t want any distractions. Thankfully \nAnydesk features a clean user interface and a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042120,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simple user experience as well as compatibility \nacross multiple computing and mobile platforms.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048160,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So even if your astronomical data set is still \nrunning on WindowsXP9, Anydesk still has you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055480,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "covered with backwards compatibility. Anydesk is \navailable for personal use, but if your research  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061200,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "requires a lot of global coordination, Anydesk \noffers custom-tailored business plans. To learn  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066840,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more about Anydesk, go to anydesk.com/spacetime \nor check out the link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1072920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. Before we go we wanted to let you \nknow, we’re celebrating the 50th anniversary of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078200,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Space Shuttle patent, with some limited \nedition merch at the merch store. There’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083440,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multicolored hoodies and t-shirts featuring \nthe original patent, the first ever space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1088380,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time notebook as well as a re-release of the \nmutli-functional space time pen in new colors.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1094320,
    "dDurationMs": 14440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can check it out at pbsspacetime.com/shop. \nThere’s a link in the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
